@@ -110,7 +110,14 @@ remembered stays its own frame (`fittedFrames`), so that it goes back when the d
 also displaces windows that are in view, and macOS itself moves and resizes some on the display that stays. What macOS does to a
 floating window then is macOS's: once Arbor is told, and after a blink, `takeFloatingWindowsFromWhereTheyAre` takes each one in view
 from where it is, by the displays that are (its place, its size, the display it belongs to), and sets no frame.
-`countAsOutOfViewWhatTheDisplaysDisplaced` says which the layout then fetches all the same: what Arbor had fitted, and what is no place.
+`countAsOutOfViewWhatTheDisplaysDisplaced` says which the layout then fetches all the same: what Arbor had fitted, and what the change
+took out of sight or out of reach (`theChangeTook`). What stays is a window from which the change took nothing, whoever put it there.
+That is worked out afterwards, from the displays alone: `CGRect.whereItMayHaveBeen` says where a window was before the screens
+changed, and the two measures there are (`areaShowing`, `titleBarIsWithinReach`) are asked of the displays as they were and as they
+are. A hand's note goes along with its display's shape for as long as no less shows than the hand left showing. That layout and no
+other: a window that could not be moved then, because its app hung or it was in the Dock, is owed nothing
+(`leaveAsItIsWhatCouldNotBeMoved`), and is fetched when its person asks, with ⌃⌥0, Show Everything, the Desktop or `relayout`
+(`isLookingForWindows`); what no display shows at all is fetched by any layout, as ever.
 For F61 the Desktop counts as not in use (`roomTerms`), and F18 is off (`soleFloatIsOn`). A command that is about a floating
 window (`float`, `grow`, `move-to-display`, `undo`) may put it anywhere on the display (`roomToFloat`).
 
